
{"id":"e807c9af-03b6-430f-a131-649ea99e1e29","object":"chat.completion","created":1785004478,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('case must be a dict')\\n    for key in ['items', 'required_failure_items', 'unresolved_without']:\\n        if key not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Missing key: {key}')\\n        if not isinstance(case[key], list):\\n            raise ValueError(f'Key {key} must be a list')\\n    items = case['items']\\n    required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\\n    unresolved = set(case['unresolved_without'])\\n    if not items:\\n        raise ValueError('items list must not be empty')\\n    tests_count = 0\\n    def test(config):\\n        nonlocal tests_count\\n        tests_count += 1\\n        cfg_set = set(config)\\n        for u in unresolved:\\n            if u not in cfg_set:\\n                return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        for r in required:\\n            if r not in cfg_set:\\n                return 'PASS'\\n        return 'FAIL'\\n    config = list(items)\\n    if test(config) != 'FAIL':\\n        raise ValueError('Initial configuration must yield FAIL')\\n    n = 2\\n    while True:\\n        L = len(config)\\n        if n > L:\\n            n = L\\n        base_size = L // n\\n        remainder = L % n\\n        parts = []\\n        start = 0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            size = base_size + (1 if i < remainder else 0)\\n            part = config[start:start+size]\\n            parts.append(part)\\n            start += size\\n        reduced = False\\n        for part in parts:\\n            if test(part) == 'FAIL':\\n                config = part\\n                n = 2\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n        if reduced:\\n            continue\\n        start = 0\\n        for part in parts:\\n            complement = config[:start] + config[start+len(part):]\\n            if test(complement) == 'FAIL':\\n                config = complement\\n                n = 2\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n            start += len(part)\\n        if reduced:\\n            continue\\n        if n == L:\\n            break\\n        n = min(L, n * 2)\\n    return {\\n        'minimal_failure': config,\\n        'outcome': 'FAIL',\\n        'tests': tests_count\\n    }\"\n}\n```","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that performs ddmin (delta debugging) on a list of items, with FAIL, PASS, UNRESOLVED outcomes. The case payload has keys: items (list of strings), required_failure_items (list of strings that must be in the failure set), unresolved_without (list of strings that if removed cause UNRESOLVED). The oracle: Given a subset of items, the outcome is:\n\n- If the subset is empty? Not specified. But ddmin typically starts with full set (all items). The initial configuration must produce FAIL. The payload has required_failure_items: these must be present in minimal failure set? Actually \"required_failure_items\" are items that are required to cause failure? The description: \"minimize a failure-inducing ordered configuration\". The oracle: given a subset, we check if the subset includes all items in required_failure_items? And also if the subset is missing any item in unresolved_without? Probably: The outcome is FAIL if the subset contains all required_failure_items; otherwise it is PASS? But there is also UNRESOLVED: If the subset excludes any item listed in unresolved_without, then the outcome is UNRESOLVED (instead of PASS). Actually the unresolved_without list: \"items that if removed cause UNRESOLVED\". So if we remove any of those, the test is UNRESOLVED. But the description says: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So the oracle: Check if the subset missing any of unresolved_without? Then UNRESOLVED. Else if the subset contains all required_failure_items then FAIL. Else PASS.\n\nBut need to verify with given fixtures:\n\nCase 0: items [d0..d7], required_failure_items [d1,d4], unresolved_without [].\nInitial full set: contains d1,d4 -> FAIL.\nIf we test subset that excludes d1 or d4 -> would that be PASS (since unresolved_without empty) or UNRESOLVED? It would be PASS because not missing any unresolved_without (none) and does not contain all required_failure_items. That matches typical ddmin.\n\nCase 2: items [d0..d7], required_failure_items [d4,d7], unresolved_without [d0]. So if we remove d0, we get UNRESOLVED. So full set contains d0 -> not missing unresolved, contains d4,d7 -> FAIL. If we test subset that excludes d4 or d7 but includes d0 -> PASS (since not FAIL). If we test subset that excludes d0 but includes d4,d7 -> UNRESOLVED because missing d0 from unresolved_without.\n\nSo the oracle function: given set S (subset of items), outcome:\n- If any item in unresolved_without is not in S: UNRESOLVED\n- Else if all required_failure_items are in S: FAIL\n- Else: PASS\n\nThat seems correct.\n\nNow ddmin algorithm: Standard ddmin as described in Zeller's paper: start with full set. Partition into two subsets (granularity 2). Test each subset. If any subset fails (FAIL), reduce to that subset (and continue with same granularity? Actually algorithm: For each subset in order, test it. If it fails, set configuration to that subset and continue testing remaining subsets? The algorithm described: \"Test subsets before complements. Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" That is the classic ddmin: test each subset; if any subset fails, set config to that subset and continue with same granularity? Wait, typical ddmin: after partitioning, test each subset. If any subset fails, continue with that subset (reduced config) and keep same granularity (n). If none fails, test complements? Actually the standard ddmin algorithm (from Zeller's paper) does: test each subset; if any subset causes failure, use that subset as new config and continue with same granularity (n). If no subset causes failure, test each complement (i.e., the original config minus the subset). If any complement causes failure, use that complement as new config and continue with same granularity. If none, double granularity (or halve? Actually when no failure, increase granularity? Wait: In ddmin, the algorithm reduces granularity (number of intervals) when a subset fails; when no subset or complement fails, it increases granularity (double number of intervals) until it reaches the size of the config (then stop). The description says: \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\"\n\nWe need to implement exactly: Partition current configuration into n subsets (n = 2 initially, then changes). For each subset i in order (list of subsets), test that subset. If outcome is FAIL, set configuration to that subset, set n = max(2, n-1?) Actually \"Decrease granularity after reduction\" means reduce n? In typical ddmin, when a subset fails, you keep the same n (or reduce?) Wait, let's re-read: \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" In many descriptions, when a subset fails, you halve the granularity (i.e., make finer?) Actually granularity in ddmin is the number of subsets. Starting with 2 subsets. If a subset fails, you reduce configuration to that subset, and you increase granularity (double the number of subsets) to explore finer details? No, typical ddmin: when a subset fails, you continue with same number of subsets? Let's recall Zeller's algorithm:\n\ndef ddmin(c):\n    n = 2\n    while True:\n        # split c into n subsets\n        subsets = split(c, n)\n        # test each subset\n        for subset in subsets:\n            if test(subset) == FAIL:\n                c = subset\n                n = max(2, n-1)  # decrease granularity? Actually n-1? Or keep same? \n                break\n        else:\n            # test complements\n            for subset in subsets:\n                complement = c - subset\n                if test(complement) == FAIL:\n                    c = complement\n                    n = max(2, n-1)  # decrease granularity?\n                    break\n            else:\n                # no failure found, double granularity\n                n = min(len(c), n*2)\n                if n == len(c):\n                    break\n    return c\n\nBut the description says \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" That aligns: after reduction (when a subset or complement fails), decrease granularity (n = n-1?) Actually typical ddmin decreases n (makes finer-grained) when a subset fails? Wait: In the algorithm above, when a subset fails, they set n = max(2, n-1) which decreases n (makes subsets larger? Actually n is number of subsets, decreasing n makes each subset larger, coarser granularity). Hmm, that seems opposite. Let's think: In ddmin, when you find a failing subset, you want to continue exploring that subset with finer granularity, so you increase n (finer split). But the algorithm I wrote does n-1? That's wrong. Let's check reference: The original ddmin (Zeller 1999) uses: n = 2, then if any subset fails, set c to that subset and set n = 2, but continue? Actually I recall: When a subset fails, you set c to that subset and then split it into 2 subsets again (i.e., n remains 2). When no subset fails, you test complements; if any complement fails, you set c to that complement and split into 2 again. If no subset or complement fails, you double the number of partitions (increase granularity) up to the size of c. Then when n reaches size of c, you stop. So the granularity adjustment is: after a reduction (subset or complement fails), reset n to 2 (or maybe decrease?). Actually the standard algorithm in many implementations: if a subset fails, continue with that subset and reset n to 2 (start over with fine granularity). But the description says \"Decrease granularity after reduction\", which might mean \"decrease the number of partitions\" i.e., make partitions larger (coarser). That seems opposite to typical ddmin. Let's parse carefully: \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" In the context of partition testing: granularity refers to the number of partitions? If you have a large number of partitions, each is small (fine granularity). If you decrease granularity, you make partitions larger (fewer partitions). After a reduction (finding a smaller failing set), you want to make partitions coarser? That doesn't make sense because you want to further refine the smaller set. Actually typical ddmin: When you find a failing subset, you continue with that subset and start again with n=2 (fine granularity on that subset). That is increasing granularity (more partitions per element? Not exactly). Let's look at the classic pseudo-code from Zeller:\n\ndef ddmin(c):\n    n = 2\n    while True:\n        # Partition c into n parts\n        parts = list(split(c, n))\n        # Test each part\n        for p in parts:\n            if test(p) == FAIL:\n                c = p\n                n = 2  # reset to fine granularity\n                break\n        else:\n            # Test complements\n            for p in parts:\n                complement = c - p\n                if test(complement) == FAIL:\n                    c = complement\n                    n = 2  # reset to fine granularity\n                    break\n            else:\n                # No failure found; double granularity\n                n = min(len(c), n*2)\n                if n == len(c):\n                    break\n    return c\n\nThat resets n to 2 after reduction. But the description says \"Decrease granularity after reduction\". That is ambiguous: if n=2, decreasing granularity would be n=1? That would be meaningless. Alternatively, \"granularity\" might be the size of each partition (or the number of parts). \"Decrease granularity\" could mean make each part smaller (i.e., increase number of parts). In common language, higher granularity means finer detail. So \"decrease granularity\" means make it coarser. That contradicts typical algorithm. But the description also says \"and otherwise double it up to current size.\" Suggesting that when not reduced, double the number of partitions. So \"decrease granularity after reduction\" could mean: when a reduction occurs, you set the number of partitions to a smaller value (like max(2, current/2)?) That would be decreasing the number of partitions, making them coarser. Let's examine the typical behavior: After a reduction, you want to examine the smaller set with finer detail, so you increase the number of partitions. But the provided description might be using \"granularity\" in the opposite sense: they say \"double it up to current size\" which is increasing number of partitions. So if doubling increases granularity (finer), then decreasing would be the opposite: fewer partitions. So after reduction, they want to set n to a smaller number, making partitions coarser. That seems weird. Let's look at the given fixture outputs? Not provided. We have to infer from the problem statement: \"Stay inside this mechanism boundary: The ddmin procedure and outcome semantics; external test execution is replaced by a deterministic oracle fixture.\" The candidate_artifact text provides more details: \"Implement `ddmin_with_unresolved` through `solve(case)` for this bounded objective: Minimize a failure-inducing ordered configuration using the registered PASS, FAIL, and UNRESOLVED semantics and exact subset/complement schedule.\"\n\n\"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements. Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\"\n\nInterpretation: Start with granularity = 2 (meaning 2 partitions). Test each partition. If any partition fails, reduce to that partition, and *decrease* granularity (i.e., make partitions coarser, e.g., n = max(2, n-1) or n = max(2, floor(n/2))?). Then continue with that reduced configuration. If no partition fails, test complements. If a complement fails, reduce and decrease granularity same way. If no failure, then double granularity (increase n, up to size of current configuration). When n reaches size of configuration, stop.\n\nBut typical ddmin decreases n (makes partitions larger) when a failure is found? Actually think about the process: Starting with full set, 2 partitions. If a partition fails, you take that partition (which is smaller) and then you want to refine it further. To refine, you would increase the number of partitions (make them smaller). But the description says \"Decrease granularity after reduction\" which would make partitions larger. That seems counterproductive. However, note that \"reduction\" means you have found a smaller failing set. The next step is to explore that set. If you decrease granularity (make partitions larger), you are combining elements, which might not help find the minimal failure. That's odd.\n\nMaybe \"granularity\" is defined as the number of elements per partition? For example, if configuration size is L, granularity g = L / n (average size per partition). Then \"decrease granularity\" means make each partition smaller (increase n). And \"double it up to current size\" means double the partition size? That would be decrease n. But the phrase \"double it up to current size\" is ambiguous.\n\nLet's look up standard ddmin: The algorithm is well-known. The typical formulation: \n- Start with n = 2.\n- Partition config into n subsets.\n- For each subset, if test(subset) fails, set config = subset and set n = 2 (reset to fine granularity). Break.\n- Else, for each subset, if test(config - subset) fails, set config = config - subset and set n = 2. Break.\n- Else, set n = min(len(config), 2*n). If n == len(config), break.\n- Repeat.\n\nThat is the common version. The phrase \"decrease granularity after reduction\" could be interpreted as decreasing the partitioning granularity back to its minimum (2) after a reduction. \"Otherwise double it up to current size\" means if no reduction, increase granularity (increase n). This matches the typical algorithm.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll adopt the typical algorithm because it's standard and the description \"decrease granularity after reduction\" might be a misnomer for \"reset to fine granularity\". Also the candidate_artifact says \"registered_call_order\" and \"one_minimal_result\" and \"unresolved_semantics\". So likely it's the standard ddmin with the unresolved twist.\n\nThus, implementation:\n\n1. Input case dict with 'items', 'required_failure_items', 'unresolved_without'.\n2. Validate fields exist, are lists, etc.\n3. Define test function that takes a list of items (ordered) and returns 'FAIL', 'PASS', or 'UNRESOLVED' based on sets.\n4. Start with full list of items as configuration (list ordered as given). The order matters? The subsets must be contiguous? ddmin typically works on ordered list, partitions are contiguous subsequences. The description says \"ordered configuration\" and \"subset/complement schedule\". The items are ordered in a list. We should treat them as ordered list; partitions are contiguous slices. That is typical for ddmin with list.\n5. So partition function: given list config and integer n, split into n contiguous parts as evenly as possible. For example, if config length L and n parts, each part has floor(L/n) or floor(L/n)+1. We'll follow standard split: first L % n parts have size floor(L/n)+1, rest have size floor(L/n). (Or as commonly done: first part size ceil(L/n), etc.)\n6. The order of testing: for each subset in order (the list of subsets), test that subset. If FAIL, set config to that subset, and reset n = 2, and break out of loop (continue while True). Then after break, go to next iteration with new config and n=2.\n7. If no subset fails, then test complements: for each subset in order, complement = config list minus that subset's elements (preserving order? The complement is the rest of the list; we need to keep the order. In list terms, remove the contiguous slice? Since subsets are contiguous, complement is two parts: left and right concatenated. So complement is config without that slice. Test complement. If FAIL, set config to complement, reset n=2, break.\n8. If no subset or complement fails, double n: n = min(len(config), n*2). If n == len(config), then break (the current config is 1-minimal).\n9. Continue until break condition (n reaches len(config) and no reduction).\n10. At end, the config is the minimal failure set (list of items). The outcome: What is the terminal outcome? The outcome of the minimal failure set (should be FAIL). Also we need to count tests: number of test executions (calls to test function). Also minimal_failure: the list of items in the minimal failure set (should be a list of strings).\n11. Return: {\"outcome\": \"FAIL\" (or might be \"UNRESOLVED\"? The initial must be FAIL, but if something goes wrong? The process ensures final is FAIL because only FAIL permits reduction, so final config must be FAIL. So \"outcome\": \"FAIL\".), \"tests\": integer count, \"minimal_failure\": list of strings.\n\nEdge cases: The unresolved_without list: if any of these are missing from the set being tested, result is UNRESOLVED. That means in subset tests, if a subset does not contain all unresolved_without items, but may still contain required_failure_items? Actually test function: first check unresolved_without: if any item in unresolved_without is not in subset -> UNRESOLVED. Else check required_failure_items: if all present -> FAIL, else PASS.\n\nBut note: The full set (initial config) must produce FAIL. That implies all required_failure_items are in full set, and all unresolved_without are also present (since full set includes all items). So initial is FAIL. That matches.\n\nWhat about the complement? Complement may remove some unresolved_without items, causing UNRESOLVED. That's handled.\n\nNow implement solve(case). case is dict. We'll extract items list, required list, unresolved list. Validate they are lists, items non-empty, etc.\n\nCount tests: increment each time test() is called.\n\nImplementation details: Use list slicing for subsets and complements. For complement: given list config and subset indices (i from start to end of contiguous slice), complement is config[:start] + config[end:].\n\nWe need to ensure we use the same ordering.\n\nReturn dict with fields: minimal_failure (list), outcome (string \"FAIL\"), tests (int).\n\nRaise ValueError if required fields missing or types wrong, or initial test not FAIL (should not happen given fixtures).\n\nNow write Python code.\n\nLet's implement in solve(case):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validation\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be dict\")\n    for key in ['items', 'required_failure_items', 'unresolved_without']:\n        if key not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing key: {key}\")\n        if not isinstance(case[key], list):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Key {key} must be list\")\n    items = case['items']\n    req = set(case['required_failure_items'])\n    unres = set(case['unresolved_without'])\n    # initial test must be FAIL\n    def test(config):\n        nonlocal tests_count\n        tests_count += 1\n        config_set = set(config)\n        # check unresolved\n        for u in unres:\n            if u not in config_set:\n                return 'UNRESOLVED'\n        # check required\n        for r in req:\n            if r not in config_set:\n                return 'PASS'\n        return 'FAIL'\n    tests_count = 0\n    config = list(items)  # copy\n    # Verify initial config yields FAIL\n    if test(config) != 'FAIL':\n        raise ValueError(\"Initial configuration must be FAIL\")\n    n = 2\n    while True:\n        L = len(config)\n        if n > L:\n            n = L\n        # compute partition boundaries\n        # split config into n contiguous parts\n        # base sizes: each part gets floor(L/n), remainder parts get extra 1\n        base_size = L // n\n        remainder = L % n\n        parts = []\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            size = base_size + (1 if i < remainder else 0)\n            part = config[start:start+size]\n            parts.append(part)\n            start += size\n        # Test subsets\n        reduced = False\n        for part in parts:\n            result = test(part)\n            if result == 'FAIL':\n                config = part\n                n = 2\n                reduced = True\n                break\n        if reduced:\n            continue\n        # Test complements\n        start = 0\n        for part in parts:\n            complement = config[:start] + config[start+len(part):]\n            result = test(complement)\n            if result == 'FAIL':\n                config = complement\n                n = 2\n                reduced = True\n                break\n            start += len(part)\n        if reduced:\n            continue\n        # No reduction, double n up to L\n        new_n = n * 2\n        if new_n >= L:\n            n = L\n        else:\n            n = new_n\n        if n >= L:\n            break\n    # final config is minimal_failure\n    minimal_failure = config  # list\n    outcome = 'FAIL'  # should be FAIL\n    return {\n        'minimal_failure': minimal_failure,\n        'outcome': outcome,\n        'tests': tests_count\n    }\n\nWe need to handle case where L may be 0? Items list should be non-empty.\n\nThat should work. Double-check with given fixtures mentally:\n\nCase0: items 8, 